        "ballot_id": "B002",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the conception and realization of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B001",
    "B028"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain optical lattice clocks as my preferred achievement. They turn precise control of atoms into a remarkably stable reference for time, advancing measurement by orders of magnitude and enabling tests of fundamental physics. The results have been refined and compared across laboratories over many years. Katori and Ye made distinct, central contributions to establishing the approach and its performance, and the case rests on durable physics as well as potential applications.",
    "case_against": "The main reservation is how to delimit the achievement and allocate credit. The field's later precision gains came from many groups, so the citation should recognize the clock concept and its decisive realization without implying that two people account for every subsequent advance. The broader clock community's contributions merit careful historical review.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson also favors Katori and Ye, while noting that the boundary between the original clock concept and later advances that brought its performance to maturity needs clarification.",
        "response": "I agree that this boundary matters. The proposed citation names the conception and realization of optical lattice clocks, rather than claiming credit for all later improvements. Katori's and Ye's distinct contributions give the award a defensible focus, while the repeated advances across laboratories establish the maturity of the underlying method."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll supports Katori and Ye for their distinct contributions but calls for careful separation of the original concept, experimental implementations, and later clock improvements.",
        "response": "That distinction strengthens the case for a concise citation centered on the lattice-clock advance itself. Continued improvement is evidence that the method is robust, but should not expand the credited discovery to every later performance gain. The independent work of Katori and Ye supports recognizing both the concept and its experimental development."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The precise historical division of credit between the initial lattice-clock concept, its first realization, and the advances that established leading performance should be checked before final attribution."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
